    private static DocumentBlock EqualityLocusSpectrum()
    {
        return new DocumentBlock.Section(H("The full equality-locus spectrum and its principal blocks"),Blocks(
            Paragraph(Text("For every real 2 ≤ a ≤ b, put c = b(b+2)−a and s = a+b+c. Then c−b = b²+(b−a) > 0, so the actual ordered global spectral theorem applies and its all-two exception is absent. In the ascending characteristic-root multiset of the same quotient C, the full statement is 0 = λ₁ < λ₂ < λ₃ = b < a+b < s < λ₄ ≤ λ₅ ≤ λ₆. The root b has algebraic multiplicity one. The following argument uses the actual characteristic product and its strict sign.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Keep the residual-quartic A,B,D,N at these parameters. Put q₃ = b−A−1, q₂ = bq₃+A+B, q₁ = bq₂−B−D, q₀ = acs(p+s), and Q₄(X) = X⁴+q₃X³+q₂X²+q₁X+q₀. The literal determinant gives h_C(X) = (X−b)Q₄(X), even without h_C(1) = 0. With q = ac, its exact value at b is Q₄(b) = −b(b+1)L, where L = (b−2)q²+b[(b−2)(b²+5b+8)+12]q+b³(b+1)(b+2)². The factorization and value identity hold for all real a,b after c = b(b+2)−a; on 2 ≤ a ≤ b all terms of L are nonnegative and its final term is positive. Hence Q₄(b) < 0.")),
            Paragraph(Text("The global application supplies h_C(X) = ∏ᵢ₌₂⁶(X−λᵢ), λ₂ ≤ λ₃ < a+b and s < λ₄ ≤ λ₅ ≤ λ₆. Since (X−b) divides h_C, b equals an actual positive root; b < s excludes the high three. If λ₂ = b, cancellation of one monic factor would give Q₄(X) = ∏ᵢ₌₃⁶(X−λᵢ). All four factors at b would be nonpositive, so Q₄(b) ≥ 0, contradicting its strict sign. Therefore λ₃ = b and λ₂ < b. Together with simple zero, positivity and the high gap this proves the full six-root order and exactly one occurrence of b. Equivalently χ_C(X) = X(X−λ₂)(X−b)P₃(X), where P₃(X) = ∏ᵢ₌₄⁶(X−λᵢ) is monic of degree three and has exactly those high roots with multiplicity.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Every proper principal block of the symmetric representative S is positive definite. To see this for any injective coordinate selector omitting a coordinate j, extend a nonzero block vector by zero on omitted coordinates. If its nonnegative quadratic form vanished, Matrix.PosSemidef.dotProduct_mulVec_zero_iff would put the extension in ker(S). Transport by the proved positive diagonal and permutation puts it in the constant kernel of C. The zero at j forces that constant to vanish, contradicting the nonzero vector. This proves the assertion for every proper principal block, including all choices of deleted supports, rather than just the two blocks displayed next.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Delete singleton support 2, which is stored index 1 in S. Order the retained supports as 13,1,3,12,23, with stored selector [4,0,2,5,3]. Its principal block B_del is [b] direct-sum T. The four-by-four T has original support order 1,3,12,23 and stored selector [0,2,5,3]. Put ρ = sqrt(a)sqrt(b)sqrt(c) > 0 and η = sqrt(a)sqrt(c). Its literal matrix is T = [[b+c+bc,−η,0,−ρ],[−η,a+b+ab,−ρ,0],[0,−ρ,c,0],[−ρ,0,0,a]]. These square-root products use the existing rootWeights, so η = sqrt(ac) and ρ = sqrt(abc) on this domain.")),
            Paragraph(Text("In the bordered order 2;13,1,3,12,23, the full S is [[a+c+ac,zᵀ],[z,B_del]] with the complete coupling vector z = (−ρ,−sqrt(a)sqrt(b),−sqrt(b)sqrt(c),0,0)ᵀ. In particular the coupling to the isolated support 13 is −ρ ≠ 0.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Exact determinant expansion gives charpoly(T) = (X−b)K(X) and charpoly(B_del) = (X−b)²K(X), where K(X) = X³−b(b²+4b+5)X²+[ac(b²+1)+b²(b+2)(b²+3b+3)]X−abc(b+3). At zero these identities also give det(T) = ab²c(b+3) > 0 and det(B_del) = ab³c(b+3) > 0. The cubic value has the exact positive decomposition K(b) = b(b+1)[a(b−a)(b−2)+ab(b−2)(b+1)+b²(b+1)(b+2)] > 0, including b = 2 and repeated parameters.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Because T is positive definite and symmetric, all its characteristic roots are real and positive. Its factorization therefore makes K a split monic cubic of degree three with positive roots κ₁ ≤ κ₂ ≤ κ₃, counted with multiplicity. Coefficient comparison gives κ₁+κ₂+κ₃ = b(b²+4b+5) > 3b, hence κ₃ > b. The product K(b) = (b−κ₁)(b−κ₂)(b−κ₃) > 0 excludes equality with b and forces κ₁ < b < κ₂: if κ₁ ≥ b the three factors are nonpositive, and if κ₂ ≤ b the first two are nonnegative and the last is negative; either case would make the product nonpositive. Thus 0 < κ₁ < b < κ₂ ≤ κ₃.")),
            Paragraph(Text("The complete cubic root multiset is {κ₁,κ₂,κ₃}; the complete T multiset is {κ₁,b,κ₂,κ₃}; and the complete deleted-block multiset is {κ₁,b,b,κ₂,κ₃}. Consequently K has no occurrence of b, T has exactly one and B_del exactly two, whereas the full quotient has exactly one as proved above. Equality between κ₂ and κ₃, or between full high roots, remains allowed.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Finally assume actual h_C(1) = 0 on this same locus. Since b ≥ 2, the root one can occupy only λ₂; every other positive position is at least b. Thus λ₂ = 1 and is simple. Exact polynomial division yields χ_C(X) = X(X−1)(X−b)P₃(X) and F(X) = (X−b)P₃(X). The monic cubic P₃ has exactly the actual multiset {λ₄,λ₅,λ₆}, each strictly greater than s. This endpoint assertion does not require a ≥ 4 or a ≥ 8. It is distinct from the earlier endpoint-comparison equality family b = a,c = (a−1)(2a−1).")),
            Paragraph(Text("The equality-locus statement and displayed block/cubic mathematics are attributed to the proposition in paper/sections/endpoint-one-spectrum.tex, lines 209–260, at b93becea662a746badeadba42fdc3d3eb3db9382. The exposition follows the completed actual spectral-product/sign and cubic-trace applications."))));
    }

